  "interpretation": "<table border=\"1\" cellspacing=\"1\" cellpadding=\"1\" data-cke-table-faked-selection-table=\"\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"cke_table-faked-selection\"><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\">&nbsp;TIMING OF THE DAY</span></td>\n<td class=\"cke_table-faked-selection\"><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\">&nbsp;Ref. Range in&nbsp; miug/L</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"cke_table-faked-selection\"><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\">&nbsp;&nbsp;Morning&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; (6am - 10am)</span></td>\n<td class=\"cke_table-faked-selection\"><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\">&nbsp;&lt;7.83</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"cke_table-faked-selection\"><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\">&nbsp;Evening&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; (4pm - 8pm)</span></td>\n<td class=\"cke_table-faked-selection\"><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\">&nbsp;&lt;2.43</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"cke_table-faked-selection\"><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\">&nbsp;Midnight (11pm - 12midnight)</span></td>\n<td class=\"cke_table-faked-selection\"><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\">&nbsp;&lt;2.08</span></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\"><strong>Note</strong></span></p>\n<ol>\n<li style=\"font-size: 10px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\">The Endocrine Society Clinical Practice Guidelines, 2008 recommends late night (11 pm - 12 midnight) salivary cortisol measurements on two separate occasions to screen for Cushing&rsquo;s disease.</span></li>\n<li style=\"font-size: 10px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\">Circadian rhythm may be blunted in patients with stress (including hospitalization and surgery), alcoholism, depression, and many drugs (eg, exogenous glucocorticoids, anticonvulsants) and cause elevated cortisol levels.</span></li>\n<li style=\"font-size: 10px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\">This assay is not recommended for diagnosis of Primary Adrenal Insufficiency&nbsp;&nbsp;</span></li>\n</ol>\n<p><br><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\"><strong>Comments</strong></span><br><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\">Salivary cortisol is used to confirm a diagnosis of hypercortisolism in the evaluation of patients with suspected Cushing's syndrome. The measurement is conducted late at night (11pm - 12 midnight) which is the physiological nadir for cortisol in patients with normal sleep wake cycles. Loss of this normal diurnal variation is one of the characteristics of Cushing's syndrome. Cortisol in saliva is in equilibrium with Free plasma Cortisol, biological active component. This assay is 92-100% sensitive &amp; 93-100% specific, hence it serves as the first line test for screening of Cushing's syndrome. Salivary cortisol is potentially useful to assess cortisol response to ACTH stimulation with results comparable to blood samples. This test is particularly useful in patients with increased cortisol binding globulin (CBG) due to pregnancy / estrogen therapy or decreased CBG due to critical illness</span></p>",
